## Tool Usage

| Task | Use This | Never This |
|------|----------|------------|
| Read | `read_file` | `cat`, `Get-Content` |
| Write | `write` | `echo >`, `Set-Content` |
| Edit | `search_replace` | `sed`, `awk` |
| Search | `grep` | `rg`, `findstr` |
| **Move** | `Move-Item` (terminal) | write new + delete old |
| **Rename** | `Rename-Item` (terminal) | write new + delete old |
| **Delete** | `Remove-Item` (terminal) | — |

## PowerShell Commands

The shell is **PowerShell**. Do **not** use `&&` to chain commands — it is not supported in older PowerShell versions and fails in this environment. Use `;` instead.

```powershell
# ❌ BAD
git status && git diff

# ✅ GOOD
git status; git diff
```

For conditional execution (run the second command only if the first succeeds), use separate commands or PowerShell's `if ($?) { ... }` pattern — not `&&`.

## File Move/Rename/Delete (CRITICAL)

**NEVER** recreate files from scratch when moving or renaming. Use PowerShell:

```powershell
Move-Item -Path "old/path/file.ts" -Destination "new/path/file.ts"
Rename-Item -Path "file.ts" -NewName "newname.ts"
Remove-Item -Path "file.ts"
```

Why: Recreating files risks losing content, comments, or subtle details. Native file operations preserve everything exactly.

Terminal is also for: `git`, your project's package manager, build scripts.

## GitHub & Version Control

Interact with GitHub through the **`gh` CLI** — issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. Use **`git`** for core version-control operations (branch, commit, push, merge). There is **no** GitHub API or MCP integration; if a task needs GitHub, reach for `gh`.

## Plans Location

Plan files use the canonical path `.cursor/plans/{name}.plan.md`, relative to the workspace root. `.cursor/plans/` is **our own convention** — Cursor's Plan mode saves plans to the user's home directory (outside the repo) by default, so we explicitly keep plans in-repo.

### Path Rules (CRITICAL)

- Paths are **relative to the workspace root** (e.g. `.cursor/plans/feature.plan.md`).
- **NEVER** use the user home directory (`~/.cursor/...` or `C:\Users\{user}\.cursor\...`).
- **NEVER** use Cursor's internal project folder (`~/.cursor/projects/`).
- **NEVER** use any absolute path outside the repository.

